Terraform state maps resources in code to real infrastructure. Critical for tracking what exists, calculating changes, and managing dependencies. Storage options: 1) Local (default) - terraform.tfstate file, good for testing only, 2) Remote backend (S3, Azure Blob, Terraform Cloud) - team collaboration, state locking, versioning. Best practices: Enable versioning, use state locking (DynamoDB for S3), encrypt at rest, limit access, never edit manually. State locking prevents concurrent modifications. Example backend config:
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "company-terraform-state"
    key    = "java-app/prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
    encrypt = true
  }
}
Multiple team members can run terraform safely with locking.

Never commit secrets to version control. Approaches: 1) Use sensitive = true attribute to hide in logs, 2) Environment variables (TF_VAR_db_password), 3) External secret management (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager), 4) Terraform data sources to fetch secrets at runtime, 5) Encrypt backend state. Example with AWS Secrets Manager:
data "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db_password" {
  secret_id = "prod/db/password"
}

resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  password = data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db_password.secret_string
}

variable "db_password" {
  type = string
  sensitive = true
}
In CI/CD, inject via environment variables. State encryption:
backend "s3" {
  encrypt = true
  kms_key_id = "arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:123456789:key/abc-123"
}
.gitignore includes terraform.tfvars, *.tfstate. Never hardcode secrets in .tf files.

Workspaces manage multiple environments (dev, staging, prod) from same configuration. Commands: terraform workspace new staging, terraform workspace select prod. Single codebase, separate state per workspace. Alternative: separate directories with own state files. Workspaces pros: single codebase, easy switching, same modules. Cons: easy to apply to wrong workspace accidentally, shared variables.tfvars, state in same backend. Separate state pros: complete isolation, different backends possible, clear separation. Cons: code duplication or complex module structure. Recommendation: Use workspaces for similar environments (dev/staging), separate state for prod. Example workspace usage:
resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  instance_type = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? "t3.large" : "t3.small"
  tags = {
    Environment = terraform.workspace
  }
}
Developers use staging workspace, prod deployments use prod workspace. Prevents accidental prod changes during development.

Providers are plugins enabling Terraform to interact with APIs (AWS, Azure, Kubernetes, Datadog). Configured in terraform block. Version management critical to avoid breaking changes. Best practice: Pin versions in terraform block:
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0" # allows 5.x but not 6.0
    }
    kubernetes = {
      source = "hashicorp/kubernetes"
      version = "2.23.0" # exact version
    }
  }
}
provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
}
terraform init downloads specified versions. Lock file (.terraform.lock.hcl) records exact versions, commit to git. Prevents team members from using different provider versions. Upgrade: terraform init -upgrade, test thoroughly. Example: AWS provider 4.x to 5.x had breaking changes in resource naming. Version pinning prevented unexpected issues.

Remote state data source reads outputs from another Terraform state, enabling infrastructure layering and team collaboration. Use cases: 1) Separate network team manages VPC, app team reads VPC IDs, 2) Shared services (RDS, ElastiCache) referenced by multiple apps, 3) Environment layering (foundation layer, platform layer, application layer). Example:
Network team (foundation):
output "vpc_id" {
  value = aws_vpc.main.id
}
output "subnet_ids" {
  value = aws_subnet.private[*].id
}

App team:
data "terraform_remote_state" "network" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "terraform-state"
    key = "network/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  subnet_id = data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.subnet_ids[0]
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.app.id]
}
Decouples infrastructure layers, teams work independently. Network changes don't require app team to re-apply unless outputs change.

Multi-layer testing approach: 1) Static analysis - terraform validate checks syntax, tflint for best practices and errors, checkov for security, 2) Plan validation - terraform plan -out=plan.tfplan, analyze plan for unexpected changes, 3) Automated testing - Terratest (Go framework) creates real infrastructure, tests, destroys, 4) Policy as code - Sentinel or OPA validates plans against policies (cost limits, security rules), 5) Integration testing - apply to ephemeral environment, run application tests, destroy. Example Terratest:
func TestVPCCreation(t *testing.T) {
  terraformOptions := terraform.WithDefaultRetryableErrors(t, &terraform.Options{
    TerraformDir: "../modules/vpc",
  })
  defer terraform.Destroy(t, terraformOptions)
  terraform.InitAndApply(t, terraformOptions)
  vpcId := terraform.Output(t, terraformOptions, "vpc_id")
  assert.NotEmpty(t, vpcId)
}
CI/CD pipeline: lint → validate → plan → OPA check → Terratest → manual approval → apply to staging → integration tests → apply to prod. Catches issues before production impact.

Cost optimization strategies: 1) Right-sizing - use variables for instance types, smaller for dev/staging, 2) Spot instances where appropriate, 3) Auto-scaling policies, 4) Scheduled scaling (scale down non-prod at night), 5) Use lifecycle rules for S3 (transition to IA/Glacier), 6) RDS reserved instances in Terraform, 7) Cost tags for tracking. Example:
variable "environment" {}

locals {
  instance_type = var.environment == "prod" ? "t3.large" : "t3.small"
  rds_multi_az = var.environment == "prod" ? true : false
}

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  instance_type = local.instance_type
  tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter = "engineering"
    Project = "payment-service"
  }
}

resource "aws_autoscaling_schedule" "scale_down" {
  count = var.environment != "prod" ? 1 : 0
  scheduled_action_name = "scale-down-evening"
  min_size = 0
  max_size = 0
  desired_capacity = 0
  recurrence = "0 20 * * *"  # 8 PM
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration" {
  rule {
    id = "archive"
    transition {
      days = 90
      storage_class = "GLACIER"
    }
  }
}
Cost monitoring with terraform-cost-estimation in CI/CD, alerts on expensive changes.

Terraform automatically determines dependencies from resource references. Explicit control: depends_on meta-argument. Implicit: referencing attribute creates dependency. Example:
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  # configuration
}

resource "aws_ecs_task_definition" "app" {
  environment = {
    DB_HOST = aws_db_instance.main.endpoint  # implicit dependency
  }
}

Explicit with depends_on:
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "attach" {
  role = aws_iam_role.app.name
  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.app.arn
}

resource "aws_ecs_service" "app" {
  depends_on = [aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.attach]  # explicit
  # ECS service needs IAM permissions attached before starting
}

Common issues: circular dependencies (can't be resolved, refactor needed), missing dependencies (race conditions). Graph visualization: terraform graph | dot -Tpng > graph.png shows dependency tree. Best practice: rely on implicit dependencies (clearer intent), use depends_on only when necessary (IAM propagation delays, external dependencies). Example: ALB must exist before registering targets, application code creation order matters (VPC → Subnets → Security Groups → RDS → ALB → ECS).

Multi-cloud uses multiple provider blocks. Challenges: different resource types, networking complexity, state management. Example payment service: primary on AWS, DR on Azure:
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
    azurerm = {
      source = "hashicorp/azurerm"
      version = "~> 3.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

# AWS resources
module "aws_app" {
  source = "./modules/aws-ecs"
  providers = {
    aws = aws
  }
}

# Azure resources
module "azure_app" {
  source = "./modules/azure-container-instances"
  providers = {
    azurerm = azurerm
  }
}

# Shared data replication
resource "aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration" "to_azure" {
  # Cross-cloud replication
}

Modules abstract cloud-specific details. Use Consul for cross-cloud service discovery. Terraform Cloud workspaces manage separate states. Considerations: data transfer costs, latency, compliance (data residency). Alternative: cloud-agnostic abstractions (Kubernetes on both clouds) reduces provider-specific code.

Provisioners run scripts on resources after creation/destruction. Types: local-exec (runs on Terraform machine), remote-exec (runs on resource via SSH), file (copies files). Last resort - Terraform docs discourage. Prefer: user-data for EC2, cloud-init, configuration management tools (Ansible). Use cases: triggering external systems, temporary workarounds. Example:
resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami = "ami-123"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"

  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "sudo apt-get update",
      "sudo apt-get install -y openjdk-17-jdk"
    ]

    connection {
      type = "ssh"
      user = "ubuntu"
      private_key = file("~/.ssh/id_rsa")
      host = self.public_ip
    }
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "echo ${self.private_ip} >> private_ips.txt"
  }
}

Better approach - user_data:
user_data = <<-EOF
  #!/bin/bash
  apt-get update
  apt-get install -y openjdk-17-jdk
EOF

Provisioners issues: not idempotent, failure handling complex, state management unclear. Use only when no alternative exists.

Multi-tool approach: 1) Checkov - scans Terraform for security/compliance (CIS benchmarks), 2) Terrascan - policy as code, 3) tfsec - static analysis for AWS, Azure, GCP, 4) Sentinel (Terraform Cloud) - policy enforcement, 5) OPA - general policy engine. CI/CD integration:
stage('Security Scan') {
  steps {
    sh 'checkov -d . --framework terraform --output junitxml > checkov-report.xml'
    sh 'tfsec . --format junit > tfsec-report.xml'
    junit 'checkov-report.xml,tfsec-report.xml'
  }
}

Example issues caught: S3 buckets without encryption, security groups allowing 0.0.0.0/0, RDS without backup, unencrypted EBS. Checkov example:
CHECK FAILED: CKV_AWS_19: Ensure S3 bucket has server-side encryption
Resource: aws_s3_bucket.data
File: /main.tf:15-20

Fix:
resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "data" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.data.id
  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

Policy as code example (OPA):
package terraform.rds

deny[msg] {
  resource := input.resource_changes[_]
  resource.type == "aws_db_instance"
  not resource.change.after.backup_retention_period >= 7
  msg := "RDS instances must have minimum 7 day backup retention"
}

Fails pipeline if policies violated, ensuring compliance before deployment.

Immutable infrastructure: never update servers, always replace. Packer builds AMIs, Terraform deploys. Workflow: 1) Packer builds AMI with application baked in, 2) Terraform references AMI, creates launch template/ASG, 3) Deploy new version: build new AMI, update Terraform variable, apply creates new instances, old destroyed. Benefits: consistent deployments, easy rollback, no configuration drift. Example:
Packer template (app.pkr.hcl):
source "amazon-ebs" "springboot" {
  ami_name = "springboot-app-{{timestamp}}"
  instance_type = "t3.small"
  source_ami_filter {
    filters = {
      name = "ubuntu/images/ubuntu-22.04-*"
    }
  }
}

build {
  sources = ["source.amazon-ebs.springboot"]

  provisioner "file" {
    source = "target/app.jar"
    destination = "/tmp/app.jar"
  }

  provisioner "shell" {
    inline = [
      "sudo apt-get update",
      "sudo apt-get install -y openjdk-17-jdk",
      "sudo mv /tmp/app.jar /opt/app/app.jar",
      "sudo systemctl enable app"
    ]
  }
}

Terraform:
data "aws_ami" "app" {
  most_recent = true
  owners = ["self"]
  filter {
    name = "name"
    values = ["springboot-app-*"]
  }
}

resource "aws_launch_template" "app" {
  image_id = data.aws_ami.app.id
}

Deploy: packer build app.pkr.hcl (creates AMI), terraform apply (uses new AMI, rolling update ASG). Zero configuration at runtime.

Prevent accidental deletion of critical resources (databases, S3 buckets with data). Mechanisms: 1) lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }, 2) deletion_protection on resources, 3) S3 versioning/MFA delete, 4) Manual approval in CI/CD for destroy, 5) Policy as code preventing destroy. Example:
resource "aws_db_instance" "prod" {
  identifier = "production-db"
  deletion_protection = true  # AWS-level protection

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true  # Terraform-level protection
    ignore_changes = [password]  # ignore password changes
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "data" {
  bucket = "company-critical-data"

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

Attempting destroy:
terraform destroy
Error: Instance cannot be destroyed
Resource aws_db_instance.prod has lifecycle.prevent_destroy

Override (requires explicit removal from code):
# Remove prevent_destroy from code
# terraform apply (updates lifecycle)
# terraform destroy

S3 bucket protection:
resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "data" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.data.id
  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Enabled"
    mfa_delete = "Enabled"  # requires MFA to delete
  }
}

CI/CD: require manual approval for any plan containing destroy operations, alert on attempts. OPA policy blocking destroys on tagged resources: Environment=production.

Drift: manual changes outside Terraform creating mismatch between state and reality. Detection: 1) Scheduled terraform plan in CI/CD (daily), 2) terraform-compliance for continuous validation, 3) AWS Config / Azure Policy detecting changes, 4) Atlantis drift detection. Reconciliation: 1) terraform refresh updates state from reality (doesn't change infrastructure), 2) terraform plan shows differences, 3) Either revert manual changes (terraform apply) or accept into state (import/update code). Prevention: 1) IAM policies restricting console access, 2) All changes through Terraform, 3) ReadOnly console access for developers, 4) Alerts on manual changes. Example drift detection pipeline:
schedule('0 6 * * *') {  // daily 6 AM
  stage('Drift Detection') {
    steps {
      dir('environments/prod') {
        sh 'terraform init'
        sh 'terraform plan -detailed-exitcode -out=plan.tfplan || true'
        script {
          def exitCode = sh(returnStatus: true, script: 'terraform plan -detailed-exitcode')
          if (exitCode == 2) {
            slackSend(message: "DRIFT DETECTED in production!", color: 'danger')
            // Create Jira ticket
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

Exit code 2 = changes detected. Example: DBA manually increased RDS storage. Drift detected, team decides whether to keep change (update Terraform code) or revert (apply Terraform).

Multi-stage pipeline with validation and safety: 1) PR stage - validate, lint, security scan, plan, 2) Dev stage - auto-apply, 3) Staging stage - auto-apply, integration tests, 4) Prod stage - manual approval, apply, verification, 5) Rollback capability. Example Jenkinsfile:
pipeline {
  agent any
  stages {
    stage('Validate') {
      steps {
        sh 'terraform fmt -check'
        sh 'terraform validate'
        sh 'tflint'
        sh 'checkov -d .'
      }
    }
    stage('Plan') {
      steps {
        sh 'terraform plan -out=plan.tfplan'
        sh 'terraform show -json plan.tfplan > plan.json'
        // Cost estimation
        sh 'infracost breakdown --path plan.json'
      }
    }
    stage('Dev Apply') {
      when { branch 'develop' }
      steps {
        sh 'terraform workspace select dev'
        sh 'terraform apply plan.tfplan'
      }
    }
    stage('Staging Apply') {
      when { branch 'main' }
      steps {
        sh 'terraform workspace select staging'
        sh 'terraform apply -auto-approve'
      }
    }
    stage('Integration Tests') {
      steps {
        sh './run-integration-tests.sh staging'
      }
    }
    stage('Prod Approval') {
      steps {
        input message: 'Deploy to Production?', ok: 'Deploy'
      }
    }
    stage('Prod Apply') {
      steps {
        sh 'terraform workspace select prod'
        // Backup state
        sh 'aws s3 cp s3://state/prod/terraform.tfstate s3://state/prod/backups/$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).tfstate'
        sh 'terraform apply -auto-approve'
      }
    }
    stage('Verify') {
      steps {
        sh './verify-deployment.sh prod'
      }
    }
  }
  post {
    failure {
      script {
        if (env.STAGE_NAME == 'Prod Apply') {
          // Automatic rollback
          sh 'terraform workspace select prod'
          sh 'git checkout HEAD~1 -- .'
          sh 'terraform apply -auto-approve'
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

Rollback: revert git commit, re-run pipeline, or restore state backup and apply previous code. State versioning enables point-in-time recovery.
